<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhawani Singh of Jhalawar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhawani Singh was Maharaj Rana of Jhalawar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> from 1899 to 1929. Known for his learning and interest in education, he founded schools and a library and built the Bhawani Natyashala <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in Jhalawar. His reign made the small state a noted centre of culture among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Rajputana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhawani-singh-of-jhalawar.jpg" alt="Bhawani Singh of Jhalawar" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jhalawar-Maharaj-Rana-Sir-Shri-BHAWANI-SINGH-Bahadur-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhawani Singh was born on 4 September 1874 to Thakur Chhatrasal of Fatehpur, a descendant of Madho Singh, the first Jhala Foujdar of the troops of Kota State. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, from 1881 to 1891, where he did well both in his studies and in outdoor sports, and then served in the Jhalawar State Police. In 1894 he married the daughter of Maharaja Durjan Sal of Kherti, under Kota State.</p><p>In 1896 the ruler Zalim Singh II was deposed as unable to govern. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> then rebuilt the state, restoring much of the land ceded by Kota in 1838, and created a new principality of Jhalawar on 1 January 1899 with a salute reduced from 15 to 11 guns. Bhawani Singh became its first chief and was invested with full powers on 6 February 1899 by Sir Arthur Martindale, the Governor-General's Agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His first years were taken up by the Indian famine of 1899–1900. He supervised the relief himself, opened shelters for the poor and brought grain from the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> to be sold below the prevailing price, and he remitted large arrears of rent. He adopted the Imperial Postal Union and British currency and weights, introduced the Nāgarī script in the courts and offices and abolished a number of petty taxes.</p><p>He supported widow remarriage and the removal of untouchability and opened schools in the countryside and for girls, where teaching and books were free. The Indian Ladies' Magazine credited him with doing more for female education than any other Indian ruler. He toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1904, meeting Carlos I of Portugal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a> and Edward VII in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and published his diary in 1912 as Travel Pictures: The Record of a European Tour. After studying the methods of the British Parliament in 1912 he introduced a constitutional government at home, while keeping considerable power for himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he placed the resources of his state at the disposal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> and held weekly lectures to counter rumours; the salute was raised to 13 guns in 1921. He brought the painter Ghasiram Hardev Sharma from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a>, who decorated the Garh Palace with murals. He died aboard the mail steamer Ranpura near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 13 April 1929, on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for treatment of heart trouble, and was succeeded by his only son, Rajendra Singh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhawani Singh of Jhalawar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bhawani Singh</b> (ruled 1899–1929) was known for his learning and interest in education, and later rulers continued to build schools and libraries.</blockquote><a href="/state/jhalawar-state">Jhalawar State</a></li></ul></section>
